<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="055" project="spaarkeai-compose-r8">
  <metadata>
    <title>FR-C03 (whole-document half) — place a whole-document change list AND its review flags by anchor, end to end, with zero text matching</title>
    <phase>5 Track C — AI Edit Placement</phase>
    <gate>blocked-on-054</gate>
    <status>pending</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Changes how whole-document revisions and review flags land in the editor — the surface where a mis-placed legal edit is most damaging (many changes at once, reviewed in bulk).</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Capability-matched: `comments[]` (review flags) is a SECOND anchor consumer that no prior task covered, and it currently overlaps with the advisory-comment path's own `sectionRef` citation resolution. Deciding whether those converge on one placement primitive or stay separate — and getting per-item failure isolation right so one bad anchor in a fifty-change list does not sink the batch — is the judgment. Placing a single anchored edit is already solved.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Same client edit path as 051/052/053/054.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>054</deps>
    <tags>frontend, compose, ai, testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>6 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Task 054 makes the whole-document review pass ABLE to return anchors. This task makes the editor place
    them, for BOTH halves of that payload — and closes the last prose-matching dependency in the AI flow.

    **`edits[]` — mostly already done, verify rather than assume.** `usePendingRedline.materializeMany`
    gained the anchor-first branch in task 051 (`resolveAnchoredSpans`, deterministic → legacy text, with an
    anchor failure REFUSING rather than falling back to a search). Confirm it holds for a REAL
    whole-document payload — many changes, mixed anchored/legacy, per-item failure isolation intact — rather
    than assuming the single-edit tests generalize.

    **`comments[]` — genuinely uncovered.** `ComposeDraftComment` is `{ target_text, comment }`: a review
    flag anchored by quoted prose, with no anchor field at all. It is the `flag-risks` intent's ENTIRE
    output (that intent emits an empty `edits` array by contract), so today the highest-volume
    whole-document capability is 100% prose-anchored.

    **The design question this task owns.** `ComposeEditor.placeAdvisoryComments` ALREADY resolves review
    findings deterministically — via `sectionRef` through the client `CitationResolver` mirror, with a fixed
    deterministic-then-legacy ordering. That is a second, older anchor path for the same kind of object.
    Decide deliberately whether `comments[]` converges onto it (one placement primitive for every anchored
    review flag) or stays separate, and justify it against CLAUDE.md §11 — "prefer one component that works
    exceptionally well over five that partially overlap". Do not leave two overlapping mechanisms by default.

    Nothing is retired here. Task 052 owns retirement, and it must not run until this task lands — a
    whole-document flag with no anchor would still depend on the search.
  </prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE frontend developer. Expert in `usePendingRedline`, `ComposeEditor.placeAdvisoryComments`, the client `CitationResolver` mirror, and the compose ledger materialize seam.</role>

  <goal>
    A whole-document revision places every edit AND every review flag at a deterministic anchor, with zero
    text matching invoked for anchored items and per-item failure isolation preserved. One placement
    primitive for anchored review flags, or a documented reason there are two.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      Per-item failure isolation is a shipped property of `materializeMany` (UAT round-4 item 1): one
      unplaceable change is skipped, recorded, and surfaced as a calm batched "N of M" banner while the rest
      apply. An anchored batch MUST keep that. Regressing it to all-or-nothing would be a worse UX than the
      prose matching it replaces, on the surface where volume is highest.

      UAT-21 is binding here: never silently mis-place, and never report `applied` for something that was
      not placed. An anchor that does not resolve REFUSES — it does not fall back to searching, because
      falling back would re-introduce the wrong-occurrence risk for exactly the items that named their
      target exactly.
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="primary-edit">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/hooks/usePendingRedline.ts</file>
      <file role="primary-edit">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeEditor.tsx</file>
      <file role="reference">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/composeCitationResolver.ts</file>
      <file role="reference">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/importedRevisions.ts (collectBlocks)</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">projects/spaarkeai-compose-r7/notes/uat-issues.md (UAT-21, UAT-24)</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project invariant 3">All placement resolves through the projection's paraId map. MUST NOT introduce a second coordinate system.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §11">If `comments[]` does NOT converge onto `placeAdvisoryComments`' resolution, answer the three-question template concretely — name the behavior that fails without a second mechanism.</constraint>
    <constraint source="UAT-21">An unresolved anchor REFUSES and reports honestly. MUST NOT fall back to a text search, and MUST NOT report `applied` for an item that was not placed.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Per-item failure isolation MUST hold: one bad anchor in an N-item batch skips that item only.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">MUST NOT delete or retire anything — task 052 owns that.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Client tests + a seam slice over a REAL whole-document payload shape, not a single-edit payload standing in for one.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>Whole-document redline materialization, advisory comment anchoring</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-049-compose-shadow-document.md</file>
      <file>docs/architecture/COMPOSE-READ-REFERENCE-FIDELITY.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/spec.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Rigor declaration">Declare rigor FULL; load UAT-21/24 and the §11 component-justification rule.</step>
    <step order="1">Verify `materializeMany`'s anchor branch against a REAL multi-change whole-document payload — mixed anchored/legacy, per-item isolation, banner counts.</step>
    <step order="2">Decide the `comments[]` question: converge onto `placeAdvisoryComments`' deterministic resolution, or justify two paths per §11. Record the reasoning.</step>
    <step order="3">Implement anchored placement for `comments[]` on the chosen path; add the anchor field to `ComposeDraftComment`.</step>
    <step order="4">Prove zero text matching for anchored items structurally (the throwing-collaborator tripwire, as in `ComposeEditAnchorPassSeamTests`), not by inspecting output.</step>
    <step order="5">Prove per-item failure isolation and honest reporting under a partially-anchored batch.</step>
    <step order="6">Confirm nothing was retired; confirm 052's remaining scope is now genuinely unreachable-in-practice for the AI flow.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If converging `comments[]` onto `placeAdvisoryComments` would regress the advisory path's shipped behavior (its fixed deterministic-then-legacy ordering, or its range-citation spanning), STOP and surface it — the two objects may be genuinely different and forcing convergence would be scope creep in the other direction.</trigger>
    <trigger>If a whole-document payload with per-item anchors breaches the ADR-040 128 KB inline cap (task 050 C-4), STOP: `ProjectComposeOutputs` SKIPS truncated entries, so the whole revision would silently vanish from the read projection rather than degrade.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/hooks/usePendingRedline.ts</output>
    <output type="code">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeEditor.tsx</output>
    <output type="test">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/hooks/</output>
    <output type="doc">projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/notes/055-review-flag-placement-decision.md</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>A whole-document `edits[]` payload places every anchored change at its paraId with ZERO text matching invoked (asserted structurally by a throwing collaborator).</criterion>
    <criterion>A whole-document `comments[]` payload (the `flag-risks` intent's entire output) places every anchored flag deterministically.</criterion>
    <criterion>Per-item failure isolation holds: one unresolvable anchor skips ONLY that item, and the banner reports honest N-of-M counts.</criterion>
    <criterion>An unresolved anchor REFUSES — it never falls back to a text search and never reports `applied` (UAT-21).</criterion>
    <criterion>The `comments[]` convergence decision is documented and justified per §11.</criterion>
    <criterion>NEGATIVE: no second coordinate system; all placement resolves through the paraId map.</criterion>
    <criterion>NEGATIVE: nothing deleted or retired (`target_text`, `match_mode`, `resolveTargetSpans` all still present).</criterion>
    <criterion>Payload size under the ADR-040 128 KB cap for a realistic whole-document revision, or the degradation stated.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>
</task>
